  "scenes": [
    {
      "narration": "One of the cleverest tools in economics is a burger. The Big Mac index, explained fast.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "hook",
          "art": "priceTag",
          "props": [
            "coin",
            "globe"
          ],
          "lines": [
            "The Big Mac",
            "index."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s1"
    },
    {
      "narration": "The idea is simple. The same Big Mac exists worldwide, so comparing its price reveals if a currency is cheap or pricey.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "truth",
          "art": "globe",
          "kicker": "the idea",
          "line": "One burger, everywhere",
          "sub": "a global price yardstick"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s2"
    },
    {
      "narration": "The Economist created it back in nineteen eighty-six.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-bigmac",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · Big Mac Index"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s3"
    },
    {
      "narration": "The same burger costs around two dollars in the cheapest country and about eight in the priciest.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "stat",
          "art": "priceTag",
          "big": "$2 vs $8",
          "label": "same Big Mac, cheapest vs priciest",
          "props": [
            "coin",
            "fire"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s4"
    },
    {
      "narration": "It's a playful version of purchasing power parity.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-ppp2",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · purchasing power parity"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s5"
    },
    {
      "narration": "If a burger is far cheaper abroad, that currency is probably undervalued against the dollar.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "stat",
          "art": "globe",
          "big": "30–60%",
          "label": "over- or under-valued vs the dollar",
          "props": [
            "chartUp",
            "chartDown"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s6"
    },
    {
      "narration": "It rests on the law of one price, identical goods should cost the same.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-loop",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · law of one price"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s7"
    },
    {
      "narration": "Even work-time tells a story. A burger costs minutes in a rich country and over an hour in a poor one.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "flash",
          "bg": "ink",
          "line": "Minutes vs an hour",
          "props": [
            "clock",
            "coin"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s8"
    },
    {
      "narration": "A simple hamburger quietly exposes which currencies are cheap and which are quietly overpriced.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "flash",
          "bg": "ink",
          "line": "A burger tells all",
          "props": [
            "coin",
            "globe"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "id": "s9"
    },
    {
      "narration": "Because its price gap mirrors the underlying exchange rate.",
      "shots": [
        {
          "block": "evidence",
          "sourceId": "wiki-exrate",
          "kicker": "Wikipedia · exchange rate"
        }
      ],
      "id": "s10"
    },
    {
